{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1773841362,"nonce":"nEvpQD9rqcz9POgv44eLSmO/XK8=","title":"Orphan detection should cover dead review sessions in waiting:review","message":"**Bug:** When a review container crashes (e.g. API 500) and exits with `waiting:review` status, the watcher's orphan detection doesn't notice because it only checks for `working`/`starting` statuses. This creates a deadlock:\n\n1. Coder container stays paused (nothing unpauses it)\n2. Review container is gone but status is `waiting:review`\n3. Orphan detection skips it → nothing restarts the review → stuck forever\n\n**Root cause:** `session_monitor.py` `maybe_resume_orphan()` line 81:\n```python\nif state.get(\"status\") not in (\"working\", \"starting\"):\n    return\n```\n\nReview sessions with `waiting:review` and no running container are invisible.\n\n**Fix:** In `check_orphaned_sessions()`, also detect review sessions (`review-*` prefix) where status is `waiting:review` but the container (`nightshift-review-\u003csid\u003e`) is gone. Auto-resume these the same way as orphaned `working` sessions.\n\nBe careful to distinguish:\n- Coder sessions in `waiting:review` — these are EXPECTED to have no container (coder is paused, review handles them). Do NOT resume these.\n- Review sessions in `waiting:review` — these should have a running review container. If the container is gone, they're orphaned and need resume.\n\n**Tests needed:**\n- Review session with `waiting:review` and no container → detected as orphan and resumed\n- Coder session with `waiting:review` → NOT treated as orphan\n- Review session with `waiting:review` and running container → NOT treated as orphan","files":null}]}